.TH NICE 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nice \- run a command with a modified scheduling priority
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nice
.RB [ \-n
.IR adjustment ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B nice
.BI \- adjustment
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B nice
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nice
adds
.I adjustment
(default 10) to the current nice value of the process, sets the
result with
.BR setpriority (2),
and then executes
.I command
with its arguments, searching
.B PATH
as
.BR execvp (3)
does.
The resulting nice value is clamped to the range \-20 to 20.
.PP
If the new nice value cannot be set (for example, a negative
adjustment by an unprivileged user), a warning is printed and
.I command
is run anyway.
.PP
With no arguments at all,
.B nice
prints the current nice value.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " adjustment"
Add
.I adjustment
to the nice value.
It must be a decimal integer, optionally signed.
.TP
.BI \- adjustment
.TQ
.BI \-n adjustment
Traditional one-argument forms of
.BR \-n .
A negative adjustment is written with two dashes, for example
.BR \-\-5 .
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
If
.I command
is run, the exit status is that of
.IR command .
Otherwise:
.TP
.B 0
The current nice value was printed.
.TP
.B 125
An adjustment was invalid or missing, no
.I command
followed an adjustment, or the nice value could not be read.
.TP
.B 126
.I command
was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
.I command
was not found.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for
.IR command .
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a build at lower priority:
.PP
.EX
$ nice \-n 15 make
.EE
.PP
Raise the priority of a command (superuser only):
.PP
.EX
# nice \-n \-5 top
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR renice (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR execve (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024.
The obsolete
.BI \- adjustment
form is a traditional extension.
